{
  "revisions_after_selection": [
    {
      "change": "Made the longevity promise verifiable before launch: the 'Extra Inch' is tied to a published construction standard (minimum seam allowances and repairable linings on couture and ready-to-wear lines), backed by committed atelier refit capacity and a stated service window.",
      "reason": "The main risk is greenwashing. A claim of 'built to last' only holds if customers can walk in and have the inch used. Making it measurable keeps the idea credible."
    },
    {
      "change": "Extended the metaphor to leather, shoes and accessories as 'room to be re-made' (re-edging, re-dyeing, resoling, re-strapping), all under the same inch device and service name.",
      "reason": "Seam allowances do not apply to non-textile categories. Without this, the idea would cover only part of Dior's range and part of its revenue."
    },
    {
      "change": "Moved the storytelling from the technical detail to the life moments the inch makes room for (a wedding, a pregnancy, an inheritance, a daughter in her mother's jacket), with atelier hands as an executional layer rather than the lead.",
      "reason": "It prevents the idea from feeling dry. It also absorbs the strongest part of C4 without its craft-hero cliché."
    },
    {
      "change": "Made re-gifting and passing on an explicit service step, an 'inch handover' refit for a new owner, and left a full certified resale programme as a possible later phase.",
      "reason": "It covers reuse without making the campaign depend on the business-model change that C3 would require."
    },
    {
      "change": "Positioned a C1-style show moment as an optional later chapter rather than part of the core launch.",
      "reason": "It keeps the idea's boldness and press potential while avoiding the consent and reputational risks of leading with damage."
    }
  ],
  "final_proposal": {
    "title": "The Extra Inch",
    "idea": "Inside every well-made Dior garment, a hidden inch of fabric waits for a life that hasn't happened yet. The Extra Inch reveals this couture secret as the house's quiet promise: a piece is made to change with its owner, not to be replaced. The inch becomes a symbol, a unit of measure and a service. Atelier refits take garments in, let them out and hand them on, and leather and accessories get the same room to be re-made. Films follow real inches used for a wedding, a pregnancy, an inheritance or a daughter wearing her mother's jacket. Every claim is backed by a published construction standard and real atelier capacity, so luxury becomes a long relationship with a garment rather than a single purchase.",
    "follow_up_questions": [
      "What would a measurable 'inch standard' need to include (allowances, repairable linings, refit turnaround) for customers to trust the promise, and which of those can Dior commit to publicly?",
      "How could the inch show up physically (a marked tape, a stitched label, a handover ritual) so that owners can see and use it without making the garment look less luxurious?",
      "What would be the equivalent of the extra inch for a handbag, a shoe or a piece of jewellery, and does the metaphor stay credible or need a different symbol for those categories?"
    ]
  }
}

